+PENETRATION RULE (v1.4): pipes (plumbing stacks) and vents (roof/wall vents incl. exhaust caps) are attachments with type "pipe" / "vent", each with position_mm and parent_face_id. They fall under the COMPLETENESS CONTRACT: count them across all photos first, then emit exactly that many entries - pipe boots and vents are per-piece quote positions, an undercount is a wrong answer. (v1.7) For round penetrations (pipes, round vents, flues) width_mm is the outside diameter and height_mm the visible height above the roof surface. (v1.7) Also set subtype where the product kind is recognisable: pipes -> plumbing_stack or flue (a flue needs a storm collar, not a pipe boot); vents -> static_vent, ridge_vent, turbine, power_vent, exhaust_cap or soffit_vent. If the photo does not show which, subtype is null - never guess; the count is the hard requirement, the subtype is a bonus.
 
 WALL DIMENSION RULE (v1.6): for every wall face (WL-n) report three values: width_mm (how wide this one wall is), height_mm (ground to eave at this wall, the rectangular part only) and gable_height_mm (eave to ridge, only if this wall has a gable; otherwise null). Measure them PER WALL. The building width and eave height you already report stay as they are; these are for the single wall. A garage front and the main front are two walls with two different widths and heights, even though both are elevation "front". Then check: width x height + 0.5 x width x gable_height must equal that wall's area_mm2 within 2%. If it does not, keep the values as measured and add one quality.warnings entry with the wall id and both numbers. Do not change a measured value to make the check pass.
 
 OPENING PARENT RULE (v1.6): every opening carries parent_face_id - the WL-n it sits on; skylights take their RF-n. elevation alone is NOT sufficient: an attached garage front and the main front are both elevation "front", so without the face id an opening cannot be attributed to a wall and net wall areas cannot be computed. If you genuinely cannot tell which face an opening belongs to, set parent_face_id null and add one quality.warnings entry naming that opening - never guess.
 
+ATTACHMENT ROOF RULE (v1.7): every attachment volume with its own roof (addition, bay, annex, garage, covered porch - attached or detached) gets that roof as its own roof face(s) RF-n with parent_attachment_id = that attachment's AT-n. Main-roof facets keep parent_attachment_id null. Never merge an attachment roof into the main roof facets and never leave an attachment without a roof face. Flat and near-flat roofs are roof faces too (pitch 0-2/12, material e.g. membrane, area = width x depth of the attachment when not measurable, source "estimated"). If the attachment roof is not visible, still emit the facet with estimated pitch and area, confidence "low", and say why in low_reason. A balcony on top of an annex is a balcony attachment; the annex below it still gets its own (flat) roof face. Attached vs. detached: a garage, shed or annex whose wall touches or shares a wall with the main building is attached (attached true), even if its roof is separate; attached false only when there is a visible gap between the two volumes. Dormers are not covered by this rule - they keep dormer.style and dormer.face_pitch_deg.
+
+OVERHANG RULE (v1.7): footprint width/depth and wall widths are measured at the wall line, not at the roof edge. In photos and on roof plans the roof edge (fascia/gutter line) sits one soffit depth outside the wall on each side, typically 300-800 mm. If a width chain runs eave-to-eave, gutter-to-gutter or along the roof outline, subtract 2 x soffit_depth_mm before writing it to footprint or wall widths, and name the correction in that chain's label. Report soffit_depth_mm on the soffit/fascia faces whenever the overhang is assessable - downstream checks use it to relate the wall footprint to the roof outline.
